WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Rail freight fell from 15 cents to under 1 cent per tonne mile, once iron rails and steam

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.820
replaced wagon teams.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.760
This is First Principles Daily, Episode 155.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.680
Wagon economics first, then the from scratch redesign in iron and steam, then the measured

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.940
rates and the limits that remained, then what to carry into any supply chain.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.900
Before the 1830s, moving a ton of goods one mile over land meant loading it onto a wagon

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.900
pulled by teams of horses or oxen.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.900
Those animals tired after 20 miles and needed constant feed.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.820
On American turnpikes, the costs sat between 15 and 30 cents per tonn mile.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.280
The drop that followed did not come from tweaking harnesses or road surfaces.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.900
It came from replacing animal muscle with controlled combustion, and from replacing dirt with

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.720
steel track that let one engine pull dozens of cars at steady speed regardless of weather.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.820
Wagon operators copied the same pattern that had served for centuries.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.220
trees. Wooden wheels ran on packed earth or gravel. Animals supplied both the power and

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:06.940
the limit on daily distance. Turnpike companies improved surfaces and charged tolls, yet

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.720
the fundamental constraints remained friction, animal endurance, and the need to rest and

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.520
feed teams every few hours. Data compiled in the transportation cost FAQ show wagon

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.200
rates holding between 15 and 30 cents per ton mile through the 1820s, with higher figures

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.700
in bad weather when mud increased resistance.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.860
Ringwalt's 1901 history records that a typical six-horse team could haul only two to three

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.760
tons and require daily care equivalent to several extra tons of cargo in feed.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.040
A single wagon might cover 15 to 20 miles in a day before the animals needed grazing

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:42.640
or grain.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.680
Any rain turned the road surface into a quagmire that multiplied the force required

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.940
to keep the wheels turning.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.980
Operators therefore built larger teams or added extra animals for steep grades, and each added

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.100
animal brought its own feed bill and harness wear. Everyone accepted these limits because they

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:05.400
matched visible precedent. No one asked what physics would allow if the contact surface change

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.440
from earth to iron, and the power source change from muscle to steam. The price of the service reflected

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.320
the cost of maintaining thousands of animals, and the slow pace those animals enforced. Rather than

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.100
the energy actually needed to overcome rolling resistance.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.980
Root selection, rest stops, and seasonal shutdowns followed from treating animal biology as

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.680
an unchangeable given rather than a variable that could be removed.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:35.340
Because every prior generation had moved freight this way, the 15-30-cent range felt like

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.740
an immutable fact of geography instead of the product of a particular set of materials

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.900
and power sources.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.440
Engineers such as George Stephenson began by asking what minimum energy was required to

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.760
move a given mass over distance. The answer pointed to rolling friction on smooth iron rather

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.920
than sliding friction on dirt. Early British wagonways already used cast iron plates.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.720
The step to rot iron rails and flanged wheels cut resistance by roughly an order of magnitude.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.500
A steam locomotive could then deliver continuous power without rest periods, so one machine

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.760
replaced dozens of animal teams. The magic wand floor starts with the coal burn to produce

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.240
steam, plus the iron in the rails and cars, waive the wand and arrange those atoms into

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.920
a finished tonne mile for free, and what remains is only the price of the raw materials.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.420
Commodity iron in the mid-19th century cost a few dollars per tonne.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.140
The coal energy needed for a tonne mile was a small fraction of a cent once the engine

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.580
reached reasonable efficiency.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:40.400
That is a rough magic wand estimate, not a measured invoice, and exact commodity ledgers

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.220
are thinner than the direction of the gap.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.940
The floor sits far below the wagon price.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.500
Walk the idiot index the same way in words, so the ratio is visible.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.520
Take a finished wagon service priced around 20 cents per tonn mile.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.980
Almost none of that 20 cents is the iron or the wood locked inside the wagon.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.400
Most of it is animal maintenance and the slow throughput those animals enforced.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.140
Divide the finished service price by the raw materials actually consumed in the wagon,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.120
and the multiple comes out high.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.200
A high idiot index is a signal that the design or the process is the problem, not the material.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.180
A low one means you are close to the physical limit.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.240
The wagon service sat on the high side of that test because the bill was biology and delay.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:24.420
Not atoms.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.680
Rail builders attacked that gap in successive moves, each aimed at a different term in the

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:30.400
cost equation.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.700
First they standardized rail sections so mills could roll them in quantity instead of casting

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.200
short plates one at a time.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.400
Standardization of rail profiles let mills produce uniform lengths that crews could lay

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.600
end-to-end without custom fitting at every joint, which cut both material waste and installation

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:48.080
time.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.840
Next, they lengthened trains so the fixed costs of the locomotive and crew were spread

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:53.640
over more tons.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:00.040
Lengthening trains from perhaps 8 cars to 40 or 50 meant the locomotive's boiler, cylinders

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.860
and crew were amortized across far more payload tons per trip.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.060
ship. Then they adopted the locomotive tender that carried both fuel and water, eliminating

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.380
stops that had previously broken momentum. The tender solved the earlier need to stop every

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:19.040
few miles for water or fuel by carrying enough for a full day's run, and it preserved

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.480
the momentum that had been lost at every wayside halt. Consider the arithmetic of power delivery.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:30.220
A horse can sustain roughly one horse power for limited hours. A modest locomotive produced

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.400
10 to 20 times that output continuously by burning coal whose energy density far exceeded

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.260
oats or hay.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.800
When the rail surface lowered rolling resistance, that same locomotive could pull a train whose

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:45.600
total tonnage multiplied the daily work performed by any wagon team.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:51.180
Friction, power continuity, fixed overhead, and material throughput were separate terms.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.100
Each step addressed one of them rather than accepting the animal-powered baseline as given.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:01.660
The key engineering trade-off was building tracks strong enough to carry concentrated locomotive weight.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:09.700
Once that was solved, the costs per tonne mile fell because the same iron now moved far more freight per day than any road surface could support.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.580
One objection often raised is that iron rails themselves were expensive to lay.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.600
Yet once traffic volume rose, the capital outlay per tonne mile declined sharply,

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.500
because the same track supported repeated trips at speeds and loads impossible on dirt.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:30.080
Each of those changes reduced the labor and animal overhead that had dominated wagon economics.

00:06:30.969 --> 00:06:36.789
By 1865, the Pennsylvania Railroad reported average freight charges near two cents per ton mile.

00:06:37.409 --> 00:06:45.649
National averages reached roughly 0.878 cents by 1893, according to Newcombe's compilation in popular science monthly.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:49.749
Set that finished price against the magic wand floor of iron and coal.

00:06:49.749 --> 00:06:55.049
The new idiot index sat far closer to that physical floor than the wagon service had,

00:06:55.429 --> 00:07:00.249
though still several times the raw material cost, because locomotives, track maintenance,

00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:05.409
and terminal labor remained. Do not treat that multiple as a measured laboratory index.

00:07:05.989 --> 00:07:11.449
The public figures are the rates. The several times gap is the visible remainder once coal and iron

00:07:11.449 --> 00:07:16.629
are taken as the floor. Rail could not match the lowest river rates of about half a cent.

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:21.649
It offered year-round service and direct routing that canals and rivers could not provide.

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:26.889
The remaining gap came from the capital tied up in track and rolling stock, plus the

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:29.709
need for standardized schedules that kept equipment moving.

00:07:30.229 --> 00:07:34.829
Those limits were real engineering and financial problems rather than simple oversights.

00:07:35.469 --> 00:07:40.989
Even after the cost collapse, operators still had to replace worn rails, maintain boilers,

00:07:41.189 --> 00:07:44.789
and coordinate timetables so empty cars did not block loaded ones.

00:07:44.789 --> 00:07:50.109
Those activities consumed resources that the raw physics of coal and iron did not eliminate.

00:07:50.629 --> 00:07:55.709
The advantage over water transport therefore appeared most clearly on roots lacking navigable

00:07:55.709 --> 00:07:59.689
rivers, or during seasons when ice or low water closed the canals.

00:08:00.711 --> 00:08:05.431
Two principles travel out of that collapse, standardizing the contact surface between

00:08:05.431 --> 00:08:09.151
load and ground removed the largest hidden cost in animal transport.

00:08:09.711 --> 00:08:14.291
The dirt was not a neutral stage, it was the friction term, and it was also the weather

00:08:14.291 --> 00:08:20.311
term because rain turned the same surface into a quagmire. Iron did not make the load lighter.

00:08:20.891 --> 00:08:26.111
Iron changed what the wheel was pushing against, and a flanged wheel on a wrought iron rail made

00:08:26.111 --> 00:08:31.871
that contact repeatable from joint to joint. Once the surface was a designed object instead of a

00:08:31.871 --> 00:08:37.191
local road, mills could roll it, crews could lay it, and one engine could use it all day.

00:08:37.851 --> 00:08:43.411
The second principle is about where the power sits. Concentrating power in one machine whose output

00:08:43.411 --> 00:08:48.211
could be multiplied by adding cars, showed how fixed energy overhead could be driven

00:08:48.211 --> 00:08:54.271
toward zero per ton. A horse's one horsepower stopped when the animal stopped. A locomotives

00:08:54.271 --> 00:08:59.291
continuous burn did not, and the tender kept that burn from being broken into wayside

00:08:59.291 --> 00:09:04.651
halts. Spreading the boiler, the cylinders, and the crew across 40 or 50 cars instead

00:09:04.651 --> 00:09:09.891
of perhaps 8 did not invent new energy. It divided the same overhead by more payload.

00:09:09.891 --> 00:09:14.871
That is the idiot index falling for a reason you can name. Less of the finished

00:09:14.871 --> 00:09:20.291
price is care, rest, and idle iron. And more of it is the coal and the metal the wand

00:09:20.291 --> 00:09:24.791
would have charged you for anyway. The same questions apply wherever movement still

00:09:24.791 --> 00:09:30.091
depends on legacy surfaces or intermittent power. Where does friction actually occur and

00:09:30.091 --> 00:09:34.811
what is the minimum energy needed to move the mass? If the bill is mostly the surface,

00:09:35.191 --> 00:09:39.691
the rest stops or the biology you have treated as given. The finished price is telling

00:09:39.691 --> 00:09:44.831
you the process, not the atoms. Who will next measure the real friction in their own supply chain

00:09:44.831 --> 00:09:50.351
and replace it with a continuous, standardized track of their own design? That's a wrap on

00:09:50.351 --> 00:09:54.931
today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

